WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.055
It's Saturday at the corner shop, and the shop sells a breakfast box.

2
00:00:07.055 --> 00:00:12.484
Inside, there's a loaf of bread, a jar of jam, and a smaller box with a packet of tea.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.306
A customer asks: what does the box cost?

4
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:19.476
And the shop gives one price, as if the box were a single thing.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.345
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:23.345 --> 00:00:26.419
A box can hold boxes, which can hold more boxes.

7
00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:29.300
So how deep does the pricing code have to go?

8
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.477
Do we need a loop for every level?

9
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.947
Let's start the way most of us would.

10
00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:36.170
Go through the box.

11
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.550
If a thing is an item, add its price.

12
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.059
If it's a box, go through that one too.

13
00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:42.989
And if that one holds a box...

14
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.046
Another loop, one level deeper.

15
00:00:45.046 --> 00:00:47.368
The code starts to look like the boxes.

16
00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:52.607
And the day someone packs one box deeper than we planned for, the price comes out wrong.

17
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:57.865
Wouldn't it be nice if a box could answer the same question as a jar of jam?

18
00:00:57.865 --> 00:00:58.991
What do you cost?

19
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.685
So let's ask the box, as if it could talk.

20
00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:04.874
We ask the big box what it costs.

21
00:01:04.874 --> 00:01:05.935
It doesn't know.

22
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:07.991
So it asks each thing it holds.

23
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.705
The bread says 2.50.

24
00:01:09.705 --> 00:01:11.060
The jam says 3.00.

25
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.426
The small box doesn't know either, so it asks its own tea.

26
00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:16.781
The tea says 4.50.

27
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.854
The small box answers 4.50.

28
00:01:18.854 --> 00:01:21.944
And the big box adds up what it heard: 10.00.

29
00:01:21.944 --> 00:01:24.828
One price, from a whole tree of questions.

30
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:25.887
Let's map it.

31
00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:27.921
Each thing is a node in a tree.

32
00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.793
An item with nothing inside is called a leaf, like the jam.

33
00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:33.762
A box is a node with children.

34
00:01:33.762 --> 00:01:39.275
And the question, what do you cost, is one function, price, that every node answers.

35
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.564
Now a bigger order: one box that holds two whole breakfast boxes.

36
00:01:44.564 --> 00:01:47.316
How much new code do we need to price it?

37
00:01:47.316 --> 00:01:48.391
Pause and guess.

38
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:52.419
None at all.

39
00:01:52.419 --> 00:01:58.601
The new box asks its two children, and each one runs the very same questions as before.

40
00:01:58.601 --> 00:01:59.383
2000 cents.

41
00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:03.220
The price function didn't even notice the extra level.

42
00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:05.217
Here's the code.

43
00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:12.397
Goods is a sealed interface: a sealed type lists all its kinds up front, so the compiler knows every case.

44
00:02:12.397 --> 00:02:15.106
An Item has a name and a price in cents.

45
00:02:15.106 --> 00:02:17.003
A Box holds a list of goods.

46
00:02:17.233 --> 00:02:18.768
And price is one switch.

47
00:02:18.768 --> 00:02:20.559
An item gives its own cents.

48
00:02:20.559 --> 00:02:23.565
A box adds up the price of each thing it holds.

49
00:02:23.565 --> 00:02:25.547
Notice that price calls itself.

50
00:02:25.547 --> 00:02:30.088
That's called recursion, and it follows the tree, however deep it goes.

51
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.168
The lesson's CompositeDemo prints the box and its price: 1000 cents.

52
00:02:36.168 --> 00:02:39.056
Then the box of two boxes: 2000 cents.

53
00:02:39.967 --> 00:02:40.799
Now the name.

54
00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:46.494
Single items and groups share one interface, and a group answers by asking what it holds.

55
00:02:46.494 --> 00:02:52.253
That's the Composite pattern: a group composed of smaller pieces that answer just like it.

56
00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:55.550
The sealed type buys one more thing.

57
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.595
Say someone adds a third kind of node, a Deal.

58
00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:03.824
The switch has no default, so the build stops at every place that prices goods.

59
00:03:03.824 --> 00:03:08.789
javac says: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values.

60
00:03:08.789 --> 00:03:12.165
kotlinc says: 'when' expression must be exhaustive.

61
00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.861
Here the box picture breaks.

62
00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:18.065
A cardboard box can't change once it's taped shut.

63
00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:19.090
A Java list can.

64
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:25.754
So say we build the small box from a list that someone else still holds, and then they add to that list.

65
00:03:25.754 --> 00:03:27.292
What happens to our box?

66
00:03:27.292 --> 00:03:28.317
Pause and guess.

67
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:39.748
Take out the copy, and the demo's last line shows the same box, with something new inside: tea 4.50, plus champagne, 30.00.

68
00:03:39.748 --> 00:03:45.380
A breakfast box already promised to a customer quietly gained a bottle of champagne.

69
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.107
Lovely for the customer.

70
00:03:47.107 --> 00:03:52.568
Less lovely when its price goes up by 30.00, and nobody at the counter touched the box.

71
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.900
The fix is one copy.

72
00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:04.982
In Java, the record's compact constructor, the short constructor that can adjust values before they're stored, copies the list with List.copyOf.

73
00:04:04.982 --> 00:04:07.642
In Kotlin, the box copies with toList.

74
00:04:07.642 --> 00:04:09.463
Now the box is taped shut.

75
00:04:09.463 --> 00:04:14.013
Edit the old list all you like: the box stays bread, jam and tea.

76
00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:16.791
A tree that can't change has two more gifts.

77
00:04:16.791 --> 00:04:20.220
It can't grow a loop, and many threads can share it safely.

78
00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:22.165
So, three rules.

79
00:04:22.165 --> 00:04:26.614
One: build the tree from copies, so it can't change behind your back.

80
00:04:26.614 --> 00:04:32.804
Two: let one function walk the tree, with no default, so the compiler checks every kind of node.

81
00:04:32.804 --> 00:04:38.995
Three: if you see checks for an item, a box, and a box in a box, that's a tree asking to be one.

82
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.067
So, how deep does the pricing code have to go?

83
00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:43.755
One level.

84
00:04:43.755 --> 00:04:45.339
An item says its price.

85
00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:47.817
A box adds up what its contents say.

86
00:04:47.817 --> 00:04:50.984
The recursion takes care of every level below.

87
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:51.945
Quick check.

88
00:04:51.945 --> 00:04:55.793
Someone adds a Deal to the sealed type and forgets the switch.

89
00:04:55.793 --> 00:04:57.407
When do you hear about it?

90
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.290
When you build, at every switch that prices or prints goods.

91
00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.611
Not on the first Saturday that a customer buys a deal.
